    "summary": "High‑energy Dark Star/Other One flow with standout Born Cross‑Eyed suite",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a scorching Dark Star that segues into The Other One, plus an intense Born Cross‑Eyed → Spanish Jam pairing. Jerry’s solos are fiery throughout while the rhythm section drives high‑energy jams. Recording quality varies, but the audience tape captures the performance clearly.",
    "review": "March 30, 1968 at San Francisco's Carousel Ballroom finds the Dead in a combustible groove. The first set launches with a haunting Morning Dew solo before diving into the classic Cryptical Envelopment → The Other One sandwich that erupts into an urgent Dark Star. That momentum rolls straight into a seamless China Cat Sunflower > The Eleven, showcasing tight interplay and daring tempo shifts. In the second set the band pulls off a rare Born Cross‑Eyed into Feedback and Spanish Jam sequence that feels both experimental and tightly locked in, followed by a soulful Death Don't Have No Mercy and two rousing Turn On Your Lovelight renditions. Throughout, Jerry Garcia’s guitar is on fire—his improvisations on Dark Star and The Other One are bold, angular, and distinct from his 1966 style—while Phil Lesh underpins the jams with solid bass foundations. Pigpen’s organ sits low in the mix but adds texture when it surfaces. The audience recording by Cotsman offers the clearest window into this high‑energy night, preserving the band’s exploratory spirit without the muffling found on some soundboard tapes.",
